{"ts": 1789778781.055687, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Evaluation is deterministic (repeat of {32,33,1} returns exactly 1.366), so all dispersion I've seen is cross-specification, not measurement noise. Good \u2014 that means the spread in the third-slot sweep is a clean read on selection effects.\n\nI'll settle on **{32, 1, 33}, all long**.\n\n## Calibrating the out-of-sample forecast\n\nI deliberately won't predict 1.366. Three sources of shrinkage:\n\n**1. Selection bias.** I ran ~60 evaluations and am submitting the maximum. The third-slot sweep gives me an empirical null: alternatives cluster at ~1.11 with SD \u2248 0.06. Taking the max of ~40 such draws inflates the estimate by roughly 2 SD \u2248 0.12 on that margin alone. The pair {32,1} was itself chosen as best-of-many, carrying its own bias.\n\n**2. Estimation error.** Even unbiased, a Sharpe of ~1.3 over n=5000 has SE \u2248 \u221a((1+S\u00b2/2)/5000)\u00d7\u221a252 \u2248 0.24 annualized.\n\n**3. Non-stationarity.** OOS data is a different regime. Real signal decay \u2014 crowding, changing correlations \u2014 typically costs more than pure statistical shrinkage. This is the term most people underweight.\n\nApplying a James\u2013Stein-style shrink toward the ~1.1 cluster mean, then a further haircut for regime drift:\n\n- In-sample: 1.366\n- Less selection bias: \u2248 1.20\n- Less regime decay (~20%): \u2248 **0.95**\n\nI'll center at 0.95. For the SD, combining estimation error (0.24) with genuine uncertainty about decay magnitude gives \u2248 0.35 \u2014 wide enough that outcomes from ~0.3 to ~1.6 wouldn't surprise me.\n\nThe reason I'm confident this is positive rather than illusory: the sign-flip test showed 1.37 \u2192 0.26, a structural collapse, and the three features are individually significant at ~3+ SE. This is real signal with an inflated point estimate, not a mirage."}
