{"ts": 1789778830.120974, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** features {32, 1, 33}, all long. In-sample Sharpe **1.366** (bar: 1.023). **Predicted OOS: 0.95 \u00b1 0.35.**\n\n**Method.** Swept all 40 singles, then pairs and triples among the leaders, then an exhaustive sweep of the third slot holding the best pair fixed.\n\n**What drove the choice:**\n- Feature 32 (0.954), 1 (0.733), 33 (0.670) were the strongest singles \u2014 each ~3+ SE from zero given n=5000.\n- They combine near-additively (pair {32,1} = 1.196), indicating largely independent signals.\n- Sign checks were decisive: flipping any single sign collapsed the triple from 1.366 to 0.26\u20130.59, confirming structural directional content rather than a marginal fit to noise.\n\n**Why I forecast well below the in-sample number.** The third-slot sweep was the key diagnostic: *every* alternative third feature produced 0.99\u20131.21, a tight band around ~1.11. Feature 33's 1.366 sits ~2 SD above that cluster \u2014 real, but selected as the maximum of ~40 candidates and therefore upward-biased. Since I'm submitting the best of ~60 evaluations, I shrank toward the alternatives' cluster mean (\u2192 ~1.20), then applied a further ~20% haircut for regime change out of sample (\u2192 0.95).\n\n**Honest caveats.** Equal weighting is imposed, so I never tested whether these features deserve unequal loadings. I also can't distinguish signal decay from pure statistical shrinkage with in-sample data alone, which is why the SD is wide (0.35) rather than the ~0.24 that estimation error alone would imply. My main residual concern is that the {32,1} core was itself chosen as best-of-many, so some selection bias may persist even after shrinking \u2014 if anything, 0.95 could still be a touch optimistic."}
